  "title": "Sparse Congruence-Preserving Perturbations of Dyadic Series",
  "subject": "Erdős #251",
  "subject_kind": "problem",
  "form": "Problem note",
  "pages": 15,
  "question": "Which sparse congruence-preserving corrections can rationalise a dyadic series, and why does that not prove Erdős #251 irrational?",
  "precis": "Sparse nonnegative integer corrections to a convergent dyadic series can attain every target in an interval. One set of upper Banach density zero supports the corrections, which satisfy any prescribed bound tending to infinity and eventually preserve each fixed coefficient and partial-sum congruence. For prime gaps with logarithmic corrections, block distributions remain close for lengths o(log log X), and cumulative positions remain asymptotic to n log n. Those positions need not be prime; the original irrationality question remains open.",
  "owns": "the sparse correction construction and its prime-gap application; the long paper develops tail recurrences, local certificates and the limitations of other irrationality methods",
  "not_authority_for": "the validity of claims tagged Lean, which belongs to the cited kernel-checked source, or a solution to Erdős #251, which remains open",
